# 90ms+90ms Confirmation & Spring Physics

Click feedback must reconcile spatial certainty with high-speed execution. TuffEx formalizes the confirmation rhythm and continuous spring dynamics.

## Why Instant Menu Dismissal Harms Usability

In many native operating systems, selecting an item from a context menu closes the panel within 0ms.
Users frequently freeze for a fraction of a second: "Did I actually click that option, or did my pointer slip outside?"
Missing feedback breaks the cognitive feedback loop.

## The Two-Beat Confirmation Rhythm (90ms + 90ms)

After a click, TuffEx items (`TxDropdownItem`, `TxContextMenuItem`) run a **two-beat confirmation rhythm of about 180ms** before the menu closes:

```
[User clicks a menu item]
  │
  ├─ [Beat 1: 90ms] The row's border, fill and shadow turn transparent, dropping the hover and focus highlight.
  │
  ├─ [Beat 2: 90ms] The row switches to TxCardItem's active state to confirm the hit.
  │
  └─ [End of beat 2] Emits the select event, then closes the menu.
```

Each beat is driven by a 90ms `setTimeout`; timers are not exact, and each beat measures about 91ms. The beats run only when the click closes the menu (`close-on-select`) and `activation-feedback` is on (both are by default); otherwise select is emitted at once. Under reduced motion, it also runs at once.

